  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Emergency Grant Aid in Response to the Flood Disaster in the Eastern Part of Libya</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">On 10 September 2023, Storm Daniel made landfall in Libya causing severe weather conditions, including strong winds and sudden heavy rainfall affecting northeastern areas. Storm Daniel&#x2019;s preliminary death toll of 5,300 in Libya surpasses the 1927 floods in Algeria (3,000 killed) as the deadliest storm in Africa since 1900. It is also the deadliest storm globally since at least 2013 when Super Typhoon Haiyan killed 7,354 people in the Philippines. The Benghazi area is one of the key destinations for internally displaced persons and migrant workers in Libya, where a large number live in informal sites.  In response, the International Organization for Migration (IOM) is supporting life-saving efforts in northeastern Libya, providing relief support directly to floods-affected populations. IOM is providing direct assistance by focusing on the distribution of non-food items, providing emergency shelters, refurbishment/establishment of collective centers and medical assistance in both displacement sites and areas of return damaged by the floods.</narrative>
  </description>
